          "title": "Constructing Fundamental Matrices & Verifying Orthogonality",
          "question": "Consider the complete graph $K_4$ with vertices $\\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ and edges $e_1=\\{1, 2\\}, e_2=\\{2, 3\\}, e_3=\\{3, 4\\}, e_4=\\{1, 4\\}, e_5=\\{1, 3\\}, e_6=\\{2, 4\\}$. Choose the spanning tree $T$ with branches $\\{e_1, e_2, e_3\\}$. 1. Write down the fundamental circuits for all chords and construct the fundamental circuit matrix $B_f = [I_\\mu \\mid B_{12}]$. 2. Construct the fundamental cut-set matrix $C_f = [C_{11} \\mid I_{n-1}]$. 3. Verify directly that $B_f C_f^T \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2$.",
          "statement": "Consider the complete graph $K_4$ with vertices $\\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ and edges $e_1=\\{1, 2\\}, e_2=\\{2, 3\\}, e_3=\\{3, 4\\}, e_4=\\{1, 4\\}, e_5=\\{1, 3\\}, e_6=\\{2, 4\\}$. Choose the spanning tree $T$ with branches $\\{e_1, e_2, e_3\\}$. 1. Write down the fundamental circuits for all chords and construct the fundamental circuit matrix $B_f = [I_\\mu \\mid B_{12}]$. 2. Construct the fundamental cut-set matrix $C_f = [C_{11} \\mid I_{n-1}]$. 3. Verify directly that $B_f C_f^T \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2$.",
