          "question": "1. Provide the complete formal proof that the Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem (Theorem 8.9) with unit capacities implies the edge form of Menger's Theorem (Theorem 4.6): in any directed multigraph, the maximum number of edge-disjoint paths from $s$ to $t$ equals the minimum size of an edge cut separating $s$ and $t$.\n2. Prove the Integrality Theorem for Network Flows: if all edge capacities $c(u, v)$ are non-negative integers, then there exists a maximum flow $f^*$ such that $f^*(u, v) \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ for all edges $(u, v) \\in E$.",
          "statement": "1. Provide the complete formal proof that the Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem (Theorem 8.9) with unit capacities implies the edge form of Menger's Theorem (Theorem 4.6): in any directed multigraph, the maximum number of edge-disjoint paths from $s$ to $t$ equals the minimum size of an edge cut separating $s$ and $t$.\n2. Prove the Integrality Theorem for Network Flows: if all edge capacities $c(u, v)$ are non-negative integers, then there exists a maximum flow $f^*$ such that $f^*(u, v) \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ for all edges $(u, v) \\in E$.",
